many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy and practice I've created  this series to provide short answers to  the most common recurring or confusing  questions about stoicism so the question  and it's a really excellent question is  what is the role of the ego or the self  in modern stoicism is it something to be  overcome or is it something to be  embraced so let me give you a very  simple answer first neither it's not  simply supposed to be embraced as it is  nor is it something that is just to be  overcome and left behind and we have to  be careful in thinking about whether  we're identifying the self with the ego  there are a lot of interesting  assumptions and even extra questions  bound up in this question about the self  where the ego and the first of them is a  matter of clarifying our terminology  what do we mean by the ego or the self  and do we actually use them to mean the  same thing that depends on what  conception we have of the ego if we're  taking something from say classic  psychoanalytic theory or some of the  spin-offs in other modern psychology  then the ego is often understood to be a  part of the self rather than the  totality of the self or the subject and  the ego is the part that we're most  aware of the part that we identify with  ego means I in Latin that's a  translation of you know the German ich  as well which is what Freud was using so  if we're thinking about the ego in that  sense we might connect that with our  self-image  with what we think we're like as opposed  to how we actually are how we do engage  with the world or the other parts of the  self so we might want to think about  which term we want to use the other  thing to keep in mind is that often ego  has this pejorative sense like you're  you know just doing things to satisfy  your ego or that's all about this  person's ego meaning it has to do with  with their self-esteem and their  perceived status in relation to other  people or their there again their  self-image which they're attempting to  preserve ask yourself is that the same  thing as the self so that's something  important to clarify from the beginning  the second thing to keep in mind is that  stoicism whether ancient or modern does  think that we have a self and it's  identified in different ways in  epictetus the pro i raices or a  translated faculty of choice or moral  purpose is in fact what you are there's  other things that you are as well but  that's at the very core of your being  it's not conceived of quite that way in  some of the other Stoics who don't use  that term but that that gives you a  sense there is a substantive self for  stoicism now whether there's an ego that  depends like I said on what we  understand by it if we're thinking about  our self-image or our consciousness of  self again the Stoics think that we  definitely have that it could be  mistaken however we can conceive of  ourselves as much better people than we  are or as having an importance that we  don't actually have or misunderstand the  way our minds and our bodies work or any  of those sorts of issues a third and  very important dimension of what's being  asked about here is those subsequent  questions should we embrace or should we  overcome the self or the ego how does  stoicism view it so if we're if we're  thinking about ego in terms of you know  self-image is something that isn't quite  the totality of what we are it might be  something to be overcome it's definitely  not something  simply to embrace you know particularly  if we have mistaken conceptions of who  we are and what kind of being we are and  our relations to other people and how  developed we are and what our values  happen to be if that's the case then we  really do want to overcome that ego do  we want to overcome the self however and  the answer is no from a stoic  perspective we're not trying to get away  from the self as a matter of fact you  can't get away from the self it's the  self that's actually deciding about the  self do we want to embrace the self that  depends on what aspects of the self  we're thinking about stoicism is in  large part about getting a good handle  on who we are surveying you know the  totality of who we are our reactions our  thoughts our views our values our  choices the history that that has made  us what we are our own responsibility in  that and so we don't want to say oh wait  we embrace all of that uncritically as a  matter of fact we may need to carve away  parts of the self that are really not  helpful or are even damaging to other  parts of the self we may have to make  some difficult decisions so stoicism is  not going to either embrace or overcome  the self entirely so what is the stoic  attitude towards the self this is a very  important fourth dimension of what's  being discussed in this question  stoicism is a teleological ethics that  means that it has an understanding of  ends or or purposes or goals  telo that thought la right this is what  we get teleological from and it thinks  that we human beings not only engage in  projects that have goals or ends but  there is also a goal or end for us to be  a fully developed fully realized human  being and that would mean to have a self  that would be you might say free of  inherent internal  traditions and conflicts a self that has  developed the virtues a self that truly  understands its place in the universe  and its relations with other people and  beings and behaves accordingly now  notice I used that word self there so  stoicism you can say has a teleological  conception of the self of what a fully  realized human nature would look like at  least an outline because this is  extraordinarily rare  but it does give us a way to make  progress in that direction and part of  the way in which we do that is not just  going on a journey but by looking within  by observing our own reactions on that  journey and making oftentimes tough  decisions about what parts we're going  to keep and what parts we're going to  try to get rid of or at least you know  put into the background so stoicism is  adopting a third stance neither  embracing nor overcoming but rather  transforming the self by the very use of  the self into what the self is supposed  to be if you found this answer helpful  for you then please share it with others  who might benefit from it this work is  entirely supported by crowdfunding on my  patreon site so if you find it valuable  consider becoming a supporter at  patreon.com slash Saddler
 

